  {
    "title": "U.S. Education Secretary Lends Support to School Choice in PA",
    "url": "http://www.repwhite.com/News/37822/Press-Releases/U-S--Education-Secretary-Lends-Support-to-School-Choice-in-PA",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "U.S. Education Secretary Lends Support to School Choice in PA\nApril 10, 2026\nHARRISBURG\n– Rep. Martina White (R-Philadelphia) partnered with U.S. Education Secretary Linda McMahon today to renew calls for Pennsylvania’s governor to opt in to a new, federal tax credit designed to expand opportunities for K-12 students.\nWhite supports Pennsylvania’s participation in a new program under which tax filers can receive a federal tax credit of up to $1,700 when they donate to approve scholarship organizations helping to cover education costs such as tuition, tutoring and school supplies. McMahon added her voice to the growing, bipartisan chorus asking for Gov. Josh Shapiro to opt in, as 27 other governors already have.\n“Secretary McMahon recognizes the importance of growing our state’s already robust options for families seeking the best educational opportunities for their children,” White said. “We’ve really set the standard for the Educational Improvement Tax Credit and Opportunity Scholarship Tax Credit programs, so it should be a no-brainer for us to take advantage of this federal tax credit developed, in large part, courtesy of our own Sen. Dave McCormick.”\nMcMahon, speaking at a press conference, emphasized that families with choices are able to address their concerns about class size, test scores, security, curriculum and learning styles.\nMore than 250,000 Pennsylvania students already attend schools outside traditional public school systems because their parents sought options that best meet their needs. Use of the federal tax credit would build on that foundation.\n“For parents, school choice is not about politics. It is about finding a learning environment where their child can succeed,” White said.\nRepresentative Martina White\n170th District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Jennifer Fitch\n717.260.6563\njfitch@pahousegop.com\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Rep. Martina White held a press conference with U.S. Education Secretary Linda McMahon to publicly urge Governor Josh Shapiro to opt Pennsylvania into a new federal tax credit program that would give donors up to $1,700 in tax credits when they contribute to organizations providing K-12 scholarship funding. The program is already active in 27 other states. No legislation was introduced or passed; this was a joint public statement calling on the governor to take executive action.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "education"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "PA Budget Invests in Working Families, White Says",
    "url": "http://www.repwhite.com/News/38254/Press-Releases/PA-Budget-Invests-in-Working-Families--White-Says",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "PA Budget Invests in Working Families, White Says\nJuly 12, 2026\nHARRISBURG – The 2026-27 state budget approved by the House of Representatives today includes several key Republican priorities, including a long-awaited cost-of-living adjustment (COLA) for retired police officers and firefighters, according to Rep. Martina White (R-Philadelphia), who voted in favor of the proposal.\nThe $50.8 billion spending plan also protects the Educational Improvement Tax Credit (EITC) program by maintaining its current funding levels despite recent proposals from Democrats to decimate the K-12 scholarship program. In addition, the budget invests an extra $10 million to help more students access career and technical education programs, expanding opportunities for students seeking workforce training and in-demand career skills.\nWhite said the final budget reflects months of negotiations that produced meaningful policy victories while limiting overall spending growth.\n“We Republicans not only successfully pushed back on some of the most dangerous and problematic initiatives from the other side, but also greatly reined in the governor’s appetite for overspending,” White said. “We negotiated this budget in a way that helps working families and does not rely on the Rainy Day fund to do so. Families across Pennsylvania continue to feel the pressure of rising costs, forcing them to make difficult financial decisions every day. They deserve a government that exercises the same discipline while pursuing policies that lower costs and help them keep more of what they earn.”\nOne of the most significant provisions in the budget is the establishment of a cost-of-living adjustment for certain retired public servants. The spending plan dedicates $170 million annually over the next 10 years to provide COLAs for eligible pre-Act 9 retirees in the police, firefighter and teacher pension systems. White said the investment recognizes the service and sacrifice of public employees whose retirement benefits have not kept pace with inflation.\n“Our retired first responders devoted their careers to protecting our communities, and many have gone years without any adjustment to help offset rising costs,” White said. “This budget takes an important step toward ensuring those who served the public with distinction receive the support they deserve.”\nWhite also highlighted the preservation of the EITC program as a major victory for families and students across Pennsylvania. The program encourages private-sector scholarship donations and has helped hundreds of thousands of children attend schools that best meet their educational needs while supporting innovative educational initiatives.\n“Protecting educational opportunity was one of my top priorities throughout these budget negotiations,” White said. “The EITC program has transformed lives by giving students access to educational options that work best for them, and I am pleased we were able to preserve this important resource.”\nWhile acknowledging that no budget is perfect, White said the final agreement represents a reasonable compromise that avoids broad-based tax increases, preserves key educational programs and delivers meaningful support for retirees and working families.\nThe budget now heads to the governor’s desk for his consideration.\nRepresentative Martina White\n170th District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Jennifer Fitch\n717.260.6563\njfitch@pahousegop.com\nwww.RepWhite.com / www.facebook.com/RepMartinaWhite\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Rep. Martina White voted in favor of Pennsylvania's $50.8 billion 2026-27 state budget, which passed the House. The budget includes $170 million per year over 10 years for cost-of-living adjustments for certain retired police officers, firefighters, and teachers; maintains current funding for the Educational Improvement Tax Credit program; and adds $10 million for career and technical education.",
      "action_type": "budget",
      "topics": [
        "education",
        "public_safety",
        "economy"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Additional Voting Time Must Be Used to Protect Crime Victims, White Says",
    "url": "http://www.repwhite.com/News/38366/Press-Releases/Additional-Voting-Time-Must-Be-Used-to-Protect-Crime-Victims--White-Says",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Additional Voting Time Must Be Used to Protect Crime Victims, White Says\nJuly 22, 2026\nHARRISBURG – House Democrats have yielded to pressure from Rep. Martina White (R-Philadelphia) and her Republican colleagues from greater Philadelphia by signaling plans to vote on legislation affecting the sentencing guidelines for second-degree murder.\nThe newly scheduled voting session Thursday, July 23, is supposed to address a measure to establish a sentencing framework for future convictions, while creating a carefully balanced parole review process for individuals currently serving mandatory life sentences.\nEarlier this year, the Pennsylvania Supreme Court ruled that the Commonwealth’s mandatory life sentence for second-degree murder, commonly known as felony murder, is unconstitutional. The Court gave the General Assembly 120 days to address sentencing for this offense through updates to the Crimes Code.\nThat deadline is Friday, July 24.\n“It is unconscionable the House adjourned for the summer without addressing this pressing matter. Victims’ families from across the state need us to take swift action so that convicted murderers are not being released from incarceration in a piecemeal manner county by county,” White said.\nAttorney General Dave Sunday estimates 1,100 inmates are currently serving life sentences after being convicted of second-degree murder. More than 500 of them are from criminal cases originating in Philadelphia.\nWhite said her hope is the House overwhelmingly approves a Senate-amended version of\nHouse Bill 1042\nto effectively address the Court’s ruling. House Bill 1042 is the logical pathway to meeting the deadline effectively and does not require the Senate to return to session before the legislation goes to Gov. Josh Shapiro.\nSecond-degree murder applies when an individual participates in certain dangerous felonies, including robbery, rape or arson, that result in another person’s death. Even if the individual did not personally cause the fatal injury, Pennsylvania law has long recognized those who willingly participate in violent felonies that lead to a loss of life must be held accountable.\nRepresentative Martina White\n170th District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Jennifer Fitch\n717.260.6563\njfitch@pahousegop.com\nwww.RepWhite.com / www.facebook.com/RepMartinaWhite\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Rep. Martina White issued a statement urging the Pennsylvania House to vote on House Bill 1042, which would establish new sentencing guidelines for second-degree (felony) murder convictions after the state Supreme Court struck down the existing mandatory life sentence as unconstitutional. The House scheduled a vote for July 23, 2026, one day before the court's 120-day legislative deadline. The bill would set a new sentencing framework for future convictions and create a parole review process for roughly 1,100 people currently serving mandatory life sentences.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "public_safety",
        "voting_rights",
        "other"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "House Democrats Must Act to Protect Crime Victims, Preserve Accountability, White Says",
    "url": "http://www.repwhite.com/News/38355/Press-Releases/House-Democrats-Must-Act-to-Protect-Crime-Victims--Preserve-Accountability--White-Says",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "House Democrats Must Act to Protect Crime Victims, Preserve Accountability, White Says\nJuly 21, 2026\nHARRISBURG – Because a court-mandated deadline is fast approaching, hundreds of men and women already convicted of second-degree murder could be resentenced and released from incarceration because of inaction from House Democrats, according to Rep. Martina White (R-Philadelphia).\n“Even though Attorney General Dave Sunday has pleaded for help with this issue, his calls have gone unanswered,” White said. “My colleagues in the House have failed to pass legislation despite instructions from the Pennsylvania Supreme Court to establish a new sentencing framework before July 24.”\nWhite joined Republican colleagues from both the House and Senate today to urge House Democrats to expediently return to session to meet the deadline and protect victims of crime.\nEarlier this year, the Pennsylvania Supreme Court ruled that the Commonwealth’s mandatory life sentence for second-degree murder, commonly known as felony murder, is unconstitutional. The Court gave the General Assembly 120 days to address sentencing through updates to the Crimes Code.\nSenate Bill 1400\npassed the Senate with bipartisan support and would establish a new sentencing structure for future felony murder convictions while creating a carefully balanced parole review process for individuals currently serving mandatory life sentences. Importantly, the bill also would strengthen protections for crime victims by ensuring they receive notice and have meaningful opportunities to participate throughout the process.\nSenate Bill 1400 has languished in the House Judiciary Committee.\nThe attorney general cautioned that approximately 1,100 inmates are serving life sentences after being convicted of second-degree murder.\n“Many of these inmates serving life sentences could seek resentencing, with some potentially becoming eligible for immediate release before a comprehensive legal process is in place. This is unconscionable,” said Rep. Joe Hogan (R-Bucks).\n“Those convicted of one of the most heinous acts should not have access to a potential loophole that would put them back on our streets,” said Rep. Kristein Marcell (R-Bucks).\nRep. Shelby Labs (R-Bucks) expressed frustration about the deadline looming without House session days on the calendar set by the Democrat majority.\n“The families of crime victims deserve accountability and the assurance that their voices will be heard,” Labs said. “Failing to act before July 24 creates unnecessary uncertainty at the expense of victims and public safety.”\nSecond-degree murder applies when an individual participates in certain dangerous felonies, including robbery, rape or arson, that result in another person’s death. Even if the individual did not personally cause the fatal injury, Pennsylvania law has long recognized that those who willingly participate in violent felonies that lead to a loss of life must be held accountable.\nRepresentative Martina White\n170th District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Jennifer Fitch\n717.260.6563\njfitch@pahousegop.com\nwww.RepWhite.com / www.facebook.com/RepMartinaWhite\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Rep. Martina White held a press conference with Republican colleagues urging House Democrats to call the legislature back into session before a July 24 deadline set by the Pennsylvania Supreme Court, which ruled mandatory life sentences for second-degree murder unconstitutional. The Senate has passed Senate Bill 1400, which would create a new sentencing framework and parole review process, but the bill is stalled in the House Judiciary Committee. Without legislative action, approximately 1,100 people currently serving mandatory life sentences for felony murder could seek resentencing without a formal legal process in place.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "public_safety",
        "other"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Northeast Philadelphia Receives $2.8 Million for Signal Upgrades, White Says",
    "url": "http://www.repwhite.com/News/38424/Press-Releases/Northeast-Philadelphia-Receives--2-8-Million-for-Signal-Upgrades--White-Says",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Northeast Philadelphia Receives $2.8 Million for Signal Upgrades, White Says\nAugust 5, 2026\nHARRISBURG – Northeast Philadelphia has been awarded nearly $2.8 million through Pennsylvania’s Green Light-Go Program, according to Rep. Martina White (R-Philadelphia). The funding will support upgrades to traffic controllers and provide new software licenses to implement next-generation transit signal priority at 140 traffic signals, including those at 53 intersections in the Bustleton corridor of SEPTA lines.\n“These upgrades will make a meaningful difference for the thousands of residents and commuters who travel through the Bustleton corridor each day,” White said. “This is an important investment that will allow us to improve safety, enhance efficiency at our intersections and ensure our infrastructure evolves with the community’s needs. These signal enhancements are an important step toward strengthening the broader transit network that supports continued progress in the 170th District.”\nThe Green Light-Go Program provides state funding to municipalities for traffic signal improvements, including equipment replacements, retiming projects, LED conversions, and technology enhancements that promote improved mobility and safety.\nRepresentative Martina White\n170th District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Jennifer Fitch\n717.260.6563\njfitch@pahousegop.com\nwww.RepWhite.com / www.facebook.com/RepMartinaWhite\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Northeast Philadelphia has been awarded $2.8 million in state funding through Pennsylvania's Green Light-Go Program to upgrade traffic signals at 140 locations, including 53 intersections along SEPTA bus routes in the Bustleton corridor. The money will pay for new traffic controllers and software to improve traffic flow and transit signal priority. Rep. Martina White announced the award but did not author the legislation creating the program.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "infrastructure"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
